            <ArticleTitle>Estradiol administration mediates the inflammatory response to formalin in female rats.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>119-22</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Female rats demonstrate higher pain sensitivity than do males in various nociceptive assays of inflammation. In the present study, we found that estradiol (20%) replacement in ovariectomized rats attenuated the chronic phase of the formalin response but only at high formalin concentrations thought to rely on peripheral inflammation. An inactive isomer of estradiol, alpha-estradiol, failed to result in the same attenuation (P &gt; 0.05). Our results suggest that estradiol's actions in inflammatory responses are mediated through genomic estrogen receptor-mediated mechanisms.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
